(window.SKILL_FULL = window.SKILL_FULL || {})["negotiation"] = [{"id": "preparing-to-negotiate", "title": "Preparing to Negotiate", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Prepare for any negotiation by fixing a clear goal, a walk-away point and a Plan B. You will also learn to map what the other side really needs before you say a word.", "sections": [{"heading": "Decide what you actually want", "body": "Most negotiations go wrong before they start, because people walk in with a feeling instead of a goal. \"I want a better deal\" is a feeling. \"I want the security deposit reduced from three months' rent to two\" is a goal you can work towards and measure.\n\nWrite down two numbers or outcomes: your ideal result and a realistic target. Say you and two friends are renting a 3BHK in Pune at ₹36,000 a month. Your ideal might be ₹33,000 with the deposit at two months; your realistic target might be ₹34,500 with the same deposit.\n\nAlso list the things that matter to you besides money: a move-in date that matches your joining date, permission to have guests, or the landlord fixing the geyser before you shift. Listing them now gives you more to work with later.", "do": ["Write your ideal and realistic outcomes in specific terms", "List non-money items that matter to you"], "dont": ["Go in with only a vague wish like \"a good deal\""]}, {"heading": "Know your walk-away point and your Plan B", "body": "Your walk-away point is the least you will accept. Below it, you politely say no. Fixing it in advance protects you from agreeing to something in the moment that you regret on the way home.\n\nYour walk-away point should come from your Plan B: what you will do if this deal does not happen. If another flat two kilometres away is available at ₹35,000 with a two-month deposit, then agreeing to ₹37,000 here makes little sense. If you have no alternative and must move in ten days, your position is weaker, and it is better to know that honestly.\n\nBefore any important negotiation, spend an hour improving your Plan B. Visit one more flat, get one more vendor quote, or ask one more colleague who could help. A stronger Plan B lets you stay calm and polite because you are not desperate.", "do": ["Fix your walk-away point before the conversation", "Strengthen your Plan B in advance"], "dont": ["Decide your limit while under pressure in the meeting", "Reveal your walk-away point early"]}, {"heading": "Map the other side's interests", "body": "A position is what someone says they want; an interest is why they want it. The landlord's position may be \"Rent is ₹36,000, final.\" Their interests may be a steady tenant for at least a year, no damage to the flat, and rent that arrives on time.\n\nGuess these interests before you meet, then test your guesses with questions. \"What has been your experience with past tenants?\" or \"What matters most to you in choosing tenants?\" often reveal more than any argument.\n\nOnce you know their interests, you can offer things that cost you little but matter to them: an eleven-month agreement instead of six, post-dated rent transfers set up on UPI autopay, or a written promise to repaint one room before leaving. Preparation is not only about your side; it is about understanding theirs.", "do": ["Separate their stated position from the reasons behind it", "Prepare open questions to test your guesses"], "dont": ["Assume the other side only cares about money"]}, {"heading": "Build a one-page prep sheet", "body": "Put your preparation on one page, or one note on your phone. It should have five lines: my goal, my realistic target, my walk-away point, my Plan B, and their likely interests. Add two or three questions you want to ask and one or two things you can offer.\n\nPlan your opening line too. For example: \"We really like the flat and can sign for eleven months. Could we discuss the rent and deposit so it works for both of us?\" This sets a friendly, problem-solving tone from the first sentence.\n\nFinally, check facts. Look at rents for similar flats in the area on two or three listing sites, and note the range. Facts make your requests sound reasonable rather than random, and they help you spot an unrealistic offer quickly.", "do": ["Write a five-line prep sheet before every important negotiation", "Research the going rate from more than one source"], "dont": ["Rely on memory when numbers are involved"]}], "takeaways": ["Turn a vague wish into a specific goal and a realistic target.", "Fix your walk-away point before you start talking.", "A stronger Plan B lets you negotiate calmly.", "Look for the interests behind the other side's position.", "Put your preparation on one page and check the facts."], "quiz": [["Which of these is a well-prepared negotiation goal?", ["\"Get the landlord to be more reasonable about the rent and deposit\"", "\"Reduce the deposit from three months' rent to two\"", "\"Win the negotiation\"", "\"Pay as little as possible\""], 1, "It is specific and measurable, so you know exactly what you are working towards."], ["What is the main purpose of setting a walk-away point in advance?", ["To share it with the other side early as a sign of honesty", "To make your first offer sound bigger", "To stop you accepting a bad deal under pressure", "To avoid preparing a Plan B"], 2, "A limit decided calmly beforehand protects you from regretting a decision made in the moment."], ["You have a written offer for a similar flat at ₹35,000. A landlord asks ₹37,000 for a comparable one. What does your Plan B suggest?", ["Paying ₹37,000 makes little sense unless it adds real value", "You should accept ₹37,000 because arguing could upset the landlord", "Plan B is irrelevant once you start talking", "You must tell the landlord they are greedy"], 0, "Your alternative sets a realistic limit; you would only pay more for extra value you actually want."], ["The vendor says, \"Payment within seven days, no exceptions.\" Which is most likely an interest behind this position?", ["Wanting to end the conversation", "Disliking your company", "A strict rule they apply to every customer without exception", "Needing steady cash flow to pay their own staff"], 3, "Strict payment terms often reflect cash-flow needs, which you may be able to address in other ways."], ["Which question best helps you uncover the other side's interests?", ["\"Is this your final price?\"", "\"What matters most to you in choosing a tenant?\"", "\"Why are you charging so much?\"", "\"Can you do better?\""], 1, "An open question about priorities invites them to explain what they truly need."], ["You must move into a flat within ten days and have no other option. What is the honest assessment?", ["Your position is strong because you are in a hurry", "Your Plan B does not matter in rental deals", "Your position is weaker, so spend time improving your Plan B", "You should hide the deadline by refusing to talk about move-in dates"], 2, "Knowing your real position helps you prepare; improving your alternatives is the best fix."], ["Which item does NOT belong on a basic negotiation prep sheet?", ["Your walk-away point", "Your Plan B", "The other side's likely interests and concerns", "A script to pressure them into deciding today"], 3, "Preparation is about clarity and understanding, not pressure tactics."], ["Why should you list non-money items, like move-in date or repairs, before negotiating?", ["They give you more things to trade and agree on", "They make the meeting longer and harder to manage", "Landlords are required to accept them", "They replace the need for a walk-away point"], 0, "More items create more possible combinations, which makes agreement easier."], ["What is a good way to check whether a rent of ₹36,000 is reasonable?", ["Ask the landlord whether it is fair", "Compare similar flats in the area on more than one listing site", "Assume all rents rise every year", "Accept the first quote you hear"], 1, "Checking several independent sources gives you a realistic range to work from."], ["Which opening line sets the most constructive tone?", ["\"Your rent is far too high.\"", "\"We'll only sign if you drop the rent today — otherwise we're looking elsewhere.\"", "\"Let's get this over with quickly.\"", "\"We like the flat and can sign for eleven months — can we discuss terms?\""], 3, "It shows genuine interest, offers something valuable and invites joint problem-solving."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The deposit conversation", "situation": "You and two friends have found a 3BHK in Hinjewadi, Pune. The landlord, Mr. Kulkarni, wants ₹36,000 rent and a three-month deposit. You have one other option at ₹35,000 with a two-month deposit, but it is farther from your office. You meet him tomorrow.", "options": [{"text": "Prepare your target (₹34,500, two-month deposit), your limit (₹36,000, two-month deposit), and ask him what he values most in tenants.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You go in with clear numbers, a Plan B behind them, and a plan to learn his interests."}, {"text": "Tell him upfront about the ₹35,000 flat and say you will go there unless he matches it.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Mentioning a real alternative can help, but leading with an ultimatum may make him defensive before you learn what he values."}, {"text": "Go in without a plan and see what he offers.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Without a target or limit you are likely to accept whatever he proposes under pressure."}]}, {"title": "No time to prepare", "situation": "A furniture rental company calls to say your requested package is ₹2,800 a month for twelve months. The sales executive asks you to confirm on the call because the offer \"may change tomorrow\". You have not checked any other company.", "options": [{"text": "Confirm immediately so you do not lose the offer.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Deciding without any comparison means you have no Plan B and no idea whether the price is fair."}, {"text": "Thank them, ask them to share the quote in writing, and say you will reply by tomorrow evening after comparing.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You buy time to research and build a Plan B without being rude."}, {"text": "Say you are not interested and hang up.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "This throws away a possibly good option before you have any information."}, {"text": "Ask whether they can reduce the price before you decide.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Asking costs nothing, but without comparison quotes you cannot judge any reduction they offer."}]}, {"title": "Reading the other side", "situation": "Your college fest committee wants a local sound vendor for a two-day event. The vendor, Mr. D'Souza, insists on 50% advance payment. Your treasurer says the college releases funds only after the event.", "options": [{"text": "Tell him your college rules do not allow advances, so he must accept it.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "This ignores his concern and is likely to end the deal."}, {"text": "Ask what the advance protects him against, then offer options such as a signed purchase order from the college or a small token advance from sponsor funds.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You explore his interest — security of payment — and offer other ways to meet it."}, {"text": "Look for another vendor who does not ask for an advance.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Having a Plan B is wise, but you may lose a good vendor without first understanding his concern."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "target", "title": "Win it before you walk in", "text": "Most negotiations are decided by preparation, not clever lines. In two minutes, learn the five things to know before any ask — rent, vendors or team decisions."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "flag", "title": "Turn a wish into a goal", "text": "A specific goal tells you what to ask for and when you've succeeded.", "bad": "I'll try to get a better deal on the flat.", "good": "Rent ₹34,500 and a two-month deposit, move-in on the 1st."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "shield", "title": "Fix your walk-away point", "text": "Decide the least you'll accept before you start. Base it on your Plan B — what you'll do if this deal fails.", "example": "Other flat: ₹35,000, two-month deposit. So I won't go above ₹36,000 here."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "refresh", "title": "Strengthen your Plan B", "text": "Spend an hour improving your alternative: one more quote, one more flat, one more helper. A good Plan B keeps you calm and polite."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "search", "title": "Find the why behind their ask", "text": "A position is what they say. An interest is why. Ask about it.", "bad": "Why are you charging so much?", "good": "What matters most to you in choosing tenants?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "bolt", "title": "Open on a friendly note", "text": "Your first sentence sets the tone for the whole talk.", "bad": "Your rent is too high. Drop it or we leave.", "good": "We like the flat and can sign for eleven months. Could we discuss rent and deposit so it works for both of us?"}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Your one-page prep sheet", "text": "Tap each line once it's written down.", "items": ["My goal and realistic target", "My walk-away point", "My Plan B", "Their likely interests", "Two questions to ask"], "tip": "Check the going rate on two or three sites first."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Take these into your next negotiation:"}]}, {"id": "creating-win-win-offers", "title": "Creating Win–Win Offers", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Build offers that give both sides more of what they value, by bringing several issues to the table and trading instead of conceding. You will learn to present options and close with a clear written agreement.", "sections": [{"heading": "Put more than one issue on the table", "body": "When only one thing is being discussed — usually price — every rupee you gain is a rupee the other side loses. That makes the talk feel like a fight. Win–win outcomes become possible when you add more issues: timing, quantity, quality, payment terms, delivery, support or length of contract.\n\nImagine your team is choosing a caterer for a 120-person office Diwali lunch. Instead of arguing only over the per-plate rate, list everything that matters: menu size, serving time, cutlery, leftover packing, advance amount and whether they can also handle the quarterly town hall.\n\nWith six issues instead of one, you can give ground where it costs you little and gain where it matters most to you. The caterer may care far more about guaranteed repeat business than about ₹20 a plate.", "do": ["List every issue that could be part of the deal", "Ask what else matters to the other side"], "dont": ["Let the whole negotiation shrink to price alone"]}, {"heading": "Trade, don't simply give in", "body": "A concession given for nothing teaches the other side to ask for more. A trade links what you give to what you get. The simplest tool is an \"if… then…\" sentence: \"If we confirm the town hall order as well, could you bring the per-plate rate to ₹420?\"\n\nLook for items that are cheap for you but valuable to them, and the reverse. For the caterer, a confirmed second booking is valuable and costs your team nothing if you need one anyway. For you, an earlier serving time may be important while it makes little difference to them.\n\nKeep a quick note of what you have offered and received. It helps you avoid giving away the same thing twice and shows you whether the overall deal is still balanced.", "do": ["Use \"if… then…\" to link what you give to what you get", "Offer things that are cheap for you but valuable to them"], "dont": ["Make one-sided concessions to seem nice", "Agree to items piece by piece without seeing the whole deal"]}, {"heading": "Offer choices, not ultimatums", "body": "People resist a single take-it-or-leave-it offer. They engage more with a choice. Prepare two or three packages that are roughly equal in value to you but differ in shape.\n\nFor example: Option A — ₹450 a plate, 40% advance, standard menu. Option B — ₹420 a plate, 25% advance, with the town hall order confirmed. Option C — ₹435 a plate, full payment after the event, with one extra dessert.\n\nTheir choice tells you what they value most, even if they pick none of them. If the caterer says, \"B looks good but I need a bigger advance\", you have learned that cash in hand matters to them, and you can adjust.\n\nKeep the options honest. Packages built to trick someone into picking a poor deal damage trust once they notice, and they usually do.", "do": ["Prepare two or three packages of similar value to you", "Treat their reaction as information"], "dont": ["Design a fake \"decoy\" option to push them into one choice"]}, {"heading": "Check fairness and write it down", "body": "Before you close, ask yourself whether the other side could explain the deal to their boss or family and feel fine about it. A deal that only you are happy with tends to come back as poor service, delays or a broken agreement.\n\nThen confirm everything in writing, even with people you know well. A short WhatsApp or email summary works: \"To confirm: 120 plates at ₹420, veg and Jain options, serving by 12:30 pm on 30 Oct, 25% advance on booking, balance within seven days of the event, town hall lunch on 15 Jan at the same rate.\"\n\nAsk them to reply \"confirmed\" or correct anything wrong. Clear written terms prevent most of the disputes that start with \"But I thought you said…\".", "do": ["Send a written summary of every agreed point", "Ask the other side to confirm or correct it"], "dont": ["Rely on a handshake for anything with money or dates"]}], "takeaways": ["Bring several issues to the table, not just price.", "Link every concession to something you receive.", "Offer two or three honest packages and learn from the choice.", "Aim for a deal the other side can feel fine about.", "Confirm every agreed point in writing."], "quiz": [["Why does negotiating only on price often feel like a fight?", ["\"Price is always the least important issue in any deal\"", "Every gain for one side is a loss for the other", "It takes too long", "People dislike numbers"], 1, "With a single issue there is nothing to trade, so the talk becomes a tug of war."], ["Which sentence is a trade rather than a plain concession?", ["\"Fine, we'll pay the higher rate.\"", "\"We can drop the dessert and the welcome drink from the menu, no problem.\"", "\"If we confirm the town hall order too, could you do ₹420 a plate?\"", "\"Whatever works for you.\""], 2, "An \"if… then…\" sentence links what you give to what you get."], ["What makes an item a good thing to offer the other side?", ["It costs you little but is valuable to them", "It is the most expensive item for you", "It is something they did not ask about and do not need", "It sounds impressive"], 0, "Low-cost, high-value items let both sides gain."], ["A vendor rejects all three of your packages but says, \"B is close, I just need a larger advance.\" What have you learned?", ["The vendor is not serious about getting your business", "Packages never work", "You should withdraw all offers", "Cash in hand matters a lot to them"], 3, "Reactions to options reveal priorities you can now work with."], ["Which is an ethical way to present options?", ["Adding a deliberately poor option so that the one you want looks better", "Offering two or three packages that are roughly equal in value to you", "Hiding the cost of one option until they choose", "Saying only one option is available today when that's not true"], 1, "Honest packages give genuine choice; tricks damage trust once noticed."], ["Why keep a note of what you have offered and received?", ["To avoid giving the same thing twice and to check the deal is still balanced", "To show the other side you are recording them", "Because it is a legal requirement", "To make the meeting longer"], 0, "Tracking trades keeps the overall deal clear for you."], ["What is a sign that a deal may not last?", ["Both sides confirmed it in writing", "It covers several issues, such as timing, price, delivery and menu", "The other side would be uncomfortable explaining it to their boss", "It took two meetings to agree"], 2, "One-sided deals often come back as poor service, delays or broken terms."], ["You have agreed the caterer's terms by phone. What should you do next?", ["Nothing; a phone agreement is enough", "Wait until the event to see what happens", "Ask a colleague who joined the call to remember all the details", "Send a written summary of all terms and ask them to confirm"], 3, "A written summary prevents \"But I thought you said…\" disputes."], ["Which of these is NOT a good extra issue to add to a vendor negotiation?", ["Payment timing", "Delivery or serving time", "Gossip about a competing vendor", "Length of the contract or number of events"], 2, "Useful issues are terms both sides can trade; gossip adds nothing and harms trust."], ["Your manager wants you to agree each point separately as it comes up. What is the risk?", ["You may give away too much before seeing the whole deal", "It is always the fastest method", "The other side will refuse to talk", "There is no risk at all"], 0, "Looking at the whole package lets you trade across issues instead of conceding one by one."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The Diwali lunch caterer", "situation": "You are organising a Diwali lunch for 120 colleagues. The caterer quotes ₹460 a plate with a 50% advance. Your budget allows about ₹430, and finance can release only 25% before the event. Your team also needs a caterer for the January town hall.", "options": [{"text": "Ask for ₹430 and 25% advance, and refuse to discuss anything else.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Two demands with nothing offered in return give the caterer little reason to agree."}, {"text": "Offer: \"If we also book you for the January town hall, could you do ₹425 a plate with 25% advance?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You trade something valuable to them — repeat business — for the price and terms you need."}, {"text": "Accept ₹460 and ask finance to find extra money.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "You give up without exploring trades and create a problem for finance."}, {"text": "Ask whether they could reduce the price if you simplified the menu.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A reasonable trade, but it gives up quality without first using the bigger lever of repeat business."}]}, {"title": "Splitting chores with roommates", "situation": "You share a flat in Bengaluru with two roommates. One, Arjun, works late shifts and wants nobody to clean or cook before 11 am. The other, Meera, wants the kitchen cleaned every morning before she leaves at 9.", "options": [{"text": "Suggest a vote and go with the majority.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Quick, but one person ends up unhappy, and their underlying needs stay unsolved."}, {"text": "Ask each person what matters most, then propose: kitchen cleaned the night before, quiet mornings until 11, and Arjun takes the weekend deep-clean.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "It meets both real interests — a clean kitchen and quiet mornings — and balances the work."}, {"text": "Tell them both to adjust and stop complaining.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Dismissing both concerns leaves the conflict to grow."}]}, {"title": "Closing the deal", "situation": "After a long call, you and a printing vendor agree on 500 brochures, ₹14 each, delivery by Friday, with payment within ten days. The vendor says, \"Don't worry, I've noted it. We've worked together before.\"", "options": [{"text": "Trust him; you have worked together before.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Even good relationships suffer when people remember terms differently."}, {"text": "Send a WhatsApp summary of quantity, rate, delivery date and payment terms, and ask him to reply \"confirmed\".", "rating": "best", "feedback": "A short written record protects both sides and the relationship."}, {"text": "Ask him to email you the final quote when he gets time.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Getting it in writing helps, but leaving it to him risks delays or missing details."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "sparkle", "title": "Grow the deal, don't fight", "text": "Win–win is not about being soft. It's about finding trades that leave both sides better off. Here are five ways to build offers people actually say yes to."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "grid", "title": "Add more issues than price", "text": "With one issue, every gain is someone's loss. Add timing, quantity, advance, delivery or repeat work — now you have things to trade.", "example": "Caterer: rate, menu, serving time, advance, town hall booking."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "refresh", "title": "Trade, don't just give in", "text": "Link what you give to what you get.", "bad": "Okay fine, we'll pay the higher rate.", "good": "If we also book you for the January town hall, could you do ₹420 a plate?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "bulb", "title": "Cheap for you, valuable for them", "text": "Look for items that cost you little but matter a lot to them — like a confirmed repeat booking you needed anyway."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "star", "title": "Offer choices, not ultimatums", "text": "Two or three honest packages invite a real conversation. Their pick shows what they value.", "bad": "₹420 a plate. Take it or leave it.", "good": "A: ₹450, 40% advance. B: ₹420, 25% advance plus the town hall order. Which suits you?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "mail", "title": "Always confirm in writing", "text": "A short summary prevents “But I thought you said…”.", "bad": "Done, we'll remember it.", "good": "To confirm: 120 plates at ₹420, serving by 12:30 pm, 25% advance, balance in seven days. Please reply “confirmed”."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Before you close", "text": "Tap each one as you check it.", "items": ["Several issues discussed, not just price", "Every concession traded for something", "They could explain it to their boss happily", "Terms written and confirmed"]}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Use these in your next deal:"}]}, {"id": "handling-no-and-counter-offers", "title": "Handling \"No\" and Tough Counter-Offers", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Respond calmly when someone says no or makes a tough counter-offer. You will learn to find the reason behind a refusal, reply with criteria instead of emotion, and walk away politely when needed.", "sections": [{"heading": "Treat \"no\" as the start of a conversation", "body": "A first \"no\" is rarely final. Often it means \"not like this\", \"not now\" or \"I don't have enough information\". If you take it personally and stop, you never find out which.\n\nRespond with curiosity: \"I understand. Could you help me see what makes this difficult?\" or \"Is it the amount, the timing or something else?\" Say you asked the facilities team for a second monitor and they said no. The reason might be budget, stock, or a policy that applies only to certain roles. Each needs a different response.\n\nListen fully before answering. Repeat the reason back in your own words — \"So the issue is that this quarter's budget is already allocated?\" — so the other person feels heard and you are sure you understood.", "do": ["Ask what is behind the \"no\"", "Repeat their reason back to confirm you understood"], "dont": ["Argue before you know the reason", "Take a refusal as a personal rejection"]}, {"heading": "Pause before you respond to a tough counter", "body": "When the other side makes a counter-offer far from what you hoped, the urge is to react at once — either give in or push back hard. Both are usually mistakes. A short pause, a sip of water or a simple \"Let me think about that\" gives you time to compare their number with your prep sheet.\n\nAvoid splitting the difference by reflex. If a vendor quoting ₹1.5 lakh for a website counters your ₹1 lakh with ₹1.4 lakh, meeting at ₹1.2 lakh may sound fair, but the real question is what the work is worth and what your Plan B costs.\n\nIn a written negotiation, such as email, it is fine to reply the next day. A thoughtful answer is worth more than a fast one.", "do": ["Take a moment before answering a big counter", "Compare their offer with your target and walk-away point"], "dont": ["Split the difference just because it feels fair", "Reply to a tough email while annoyed"]}, {"heading": "Answer with criteria, not pressure", "body": "When you push back, base it on something both of you can accept as fair: market rates, quotes from other suppliers, past agreements, company policy or the scope of work. \"Two other agencies quoted between ₹1–1.15 lakh for the same five-page site\" is stronger than \"That's too expensive.\"\n\nThen make a conditional counter rather than a flat demand. \"If we provide all the content and photos ourselves, could you do it for ₹1.1 lakh?\" This keeps the talk moving and shows you are willing to give something.\n\nIf they hold firm on price, change the shape of the deal instead: fewer pages now and more later, a longer timeline, or payment in milestones. A deal that works for both often looks different from either side's first offer.", "do": ["Use objective facts to explain your position", "Make conditional counters that offer something in return"], "dont": ["Call their offer unfair or greedy", "Repeat the same demand louder"]}, {"heading": "Know when and how to walk away", "body": "Sometimes the best outcome is no deal. If the offer stays below your walk-away point and your Plan B is better, saying no is the sensible choice — not a failure.\n\nWalk away politely and leave the door open: \"Thank you for your time. At ₹1.4 lakh we can't go ahead, but if anything changes on your side, please do get in touch.\" People sometimes come back days later with a better offer, and even if they don't, you keep a good relationship for the future.\n\nNever use a fake walk-away as a tactic. Claiming you have a better offer that doesn't exist is dishonest, and if the other side calls your bluff you lose both credibility and the deal.", "do": ["Leave politely and keep the door open", "Walk away only when your Plan B is genuinely better"], "dont": ["Bluff about offers you do not have", "Burn bridges with an angry exit"]}], "takeaways": ["A first \"no\" usually means \"not like this\" — ask why.", "Pause before replying to a tough counter-offer.", "Push back with fair criteria, not pressure.", "Make conditional counters and change the shape of the deal.", "Walk away politely when your Plan B is genuinely better."], "quiz": [["Your request for a second monitor is refused. What is the best first response?", ["\"That's unfair; others have two.\"", "\"Okay, never mind.\"", "\"I'll escalate this to my manager and get it approved.\"", "\"I understand. Is it budget, stock or policy?\""], 3, "Finding the reason tells you which solution might work."], ["Why repeat the other person's reason back in your own words?", ["It confirms you understood and shows you listened", "It delays the conversation and buys you time to think of a reply", "It is a pressure tactic", "It proves they were wrong"], 0, "Paraphrasing builds understanding and makes the other person feel heard."], ["A vendor counters your ₹1 lakh with ₹1.4 lakh. What should guide your next move?", ["Meeting exactly halfway between the two numbers", "Your target, walk-away point and Plan B", "How tired you are", "Whatever ends the call fastest"], 1, "Your preparation, not a reflex to split the difference, should drive the reply."], ["Which pushback is based on fair criteria?", ["\"That's far too expensive for a simple five-page website, honestly.\"", "\"You're trying to cheat us.\"", "\"Two other agencies quoted ₹1–1.15 lakh for the same scope.\"", "\"My boss will be angry.\""], 2, "Comparable quotes give both sides an objective reference point."], ["Which is a conditional counter-offer?", ["\"₹1 lakh, final.\"", "\"If we supply the content and photos, could you do it for ₹1.1 lakh?\"", "\"Please reduce it.\"", "\"Others would do it cheaper.\""], 1, "It links your request to something you will give in return."], ["The vendor will not move on price. What is a useful next step?", ["Change the shape of the deal: fewer pages now, milestones or a longer timeline", "Keep repeating your number", "End the talk rudely", "Accept immediately"], 0, "Changing scope or terms can create a deal that works when price is stuck."], ["A tough counter-offer arrives by email while you are annoyed. What should you do?", ["Reply immediately and firmly to show you are serious about price", "Forward it to colleagues with a sarcastic comment", "Ignore it permanently", "Wait, check it against your prep sheet and reply calmly later"], 3, "Written negotiations allow time; a calm reply is more effective."], ["When is walking away the sensible choice?", ["Whenever the other side says no once", "When the offer stays below your limit and your Plan B is better", "When you feel nervous", "Never; any deal at all is better than walking away with nothing"], 1, "No deal is better than a deal worse than your real alternative."], ["Why is bluffing about a better offer a bad idea?", ["It is too complicated", "Vendors almost never ask about other offers, so it can't be checked", "If your bluff is called, you lose credibility and possibly the deal", "It always works too well"], 2, "Dishonesty risks both the relationship and the outcome."], ["Which exit line best keeps the relationship intact?", ["\"Thanks for your time. We can't go ahead at this price, but do stay in touch.\"", "\"Forget it, you're too expensive.\"", "\"We'll never work with you.\"", "Leaving the meeting without saying anything"], 0, "A polite, open-door exit preserves goodwill for the future."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The website quote", "situation": "Your small team at a Jaipur-based startup needs a five-page website. Your budget is ₹1.1 lakh. The agency you like quoted ₹1.5 lakh, and when you offered ₹1 lakh they countered with ₹1.4 lakh. Two other agencies quoted ₹1–1.15 lakh, but their portfolios are weaker.", "options": [{"text": "Suggest meeting halfway at ₹1.2 lakh to close it quickly.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "It may close the deal, but it goes above your budget and ignores the market data you have."}, {"text": "Share the other quotes and ask, \"If we provide all content and photos and pay in three milestones, could you do ₹1.1 lakh?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You use fair criteria and offer something in return, keeping the agency you prefer in play."}, {"text": "Tell them their price is ridiculous and you're going elsewhere.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Insulting the offer damages the relationship and closes a door you might want open."}]}, {"title": "No to work-from-home", "situation": "You asked your team lead, Ms. Fernandes, for two work-from-home days a week because of a long commute in Mumbai. She said, \"No, we need people in the office.\" You are disappointed.", "options": [{"text": "Accept it and never raise it again.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "You lose the chance to understand her concern or find a middle path."}, {"text": "Ask what makes office presence important for the team, then propose a one-month trial of one WFH day with clear deliverables.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You uncover her interest and offer a low-risk option that addresses it."}, {"text": "Point out that other teams allow WFH.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Comparisons without understanding her reasons can feel like pressure."}, {"text": "Ask whether you could shift your hours to avoid peak traffic instead.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A creative alternative, but you have not yet learned why she said no."}]}, {"title": "Landlord's surprise increase", "situation": "Your landlord in Hyderabad informs you that rent will rise from ₹18,000 to ₹21,000 at renewal next month. Similar flats nearby are listed at ₹19,000–20,000. You'd prefer to stay, but you have seen one flat you could move to.", "options": [{"text": "Pay ₹21,000 to avoid the hassle of moving.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Staying has real value, but you give up a reasonable chance to agree a fairer rent."}, {"text": "Share the nearby listings, mention you've been a reliable tenant, and propose ₹19,500 for an eleven-month renewal.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You use market facts and your track record, and offer something he values — a renewed contract."}, {"text": "Tell him you have found a cheaper flat at ₹17,000, even though you haven't.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Bluffing is dishonest and could backfire if he simply lets you go."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "refresh", "title": "\"No\" isn't the end", "text": "A first no usually means “not like this” or “not now”. Learn how to find the reason, answer tough counters calmly and walk away gracefully when you need to."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "search", "title": "Ask what's behind the no", "text": "Different reasons need different answers — budget, timing and policy each have their own fix.", "bad": "That's unfair, others have two monitors.", "good": "I understand. Is it budget, stock or policy that makes it difficult?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "clock", "title": "Pause before you reply", "text": "A big counter triggers a reflex to cave or fight. Say “Let me think about that”, check your prep sheet, and don't split the difference just because it feels fair."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "chart", "title": "Push back with facts", "text": "Fair criteria — market rates, other quotes, scope — are hard to argue with.", "bad": "That's way too expensive.", "good": "Two other agencies quoted ₹1–1.15 lakh for the same five pages."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "bolt", "title": "Counter with a condition", "text": "Offer something in return so the talk keeps moving.", "bad": "₹1 lakh. Final.", "good": "If we supply all the content and pay in milestones, could you do ₹1.1 lakh?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "grid", "title": "Stuck on price? Change the shape", "text": "Fewer pages now and more later, a longer timeline, or milestone payments can unlock a deal when the number won't move."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "flag", "title": "Walk away, leave the door open", "text": "No deal beats a bad deal — but exit politely. Never bluff about offers you don't have.", "bad": "Forget it, you're too expensive.", "good": "Thank you for your time. We can't go ahead at this price, but please get in touch if anything changes."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Next time you hear no, remember:"}]}, {"id": "persuading-without-pressure", "title": "Persuading Without Pressure", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Persuade colleagues, friends and clients by linking your idea to their goals, backing it with evidence and respecting their right to say no. You will learn to spot and avoid manipulative tactics.", "sections": [{"heading": "Start with what they care about", "body": "People are persuaded by reasons that matter to them, not by reasons that matter to you. Before you pitch an idea, ask yourself: what is this person trying to achieve, and what are they worried about?\n\nSuppose you want your team to move weekly reports from emailed Excel files to a shared Google Sheet. If your manager cares about accuracy, lead with that: \"A shared sheet means everyone edits one version, so we stop sending wrong numbers to the regional office.\" If a colleague's concern is extra work, address it directly: \"Setup takes one afternoon, and after that it saves each of us about an hour a week.\"\n\nThe idea is the same; the reason you lead with changes with the listener. This is not trickery — it is explaining honestly why the idea is good for them.", "do": ["Lead with the listener's goal or worry", "Explain the benefit in their terms"], "dont": ["Open with why the idea is good for you"]}, {"heading": "Show evidence and make it easy to try", "body": "Opinions are easy to dismiss; evidence is harder. Use specific numbers, a short example or a comparison: \"Last month we sent two reports with mismatched totals; both came from people working on old copies.\" One concrete example often persuades more than five general claims.\n\nThen lower the risk of saying yes. Suggest a small trial: \"Can we use the shared sheet for two weeks and review it at the next team meeting?\" A trial is easier to agree to than a permanent change, and it lets the results do the persuading.\n\nBe honest about the downsides too. \"It may feel slower for the first few days while we get used to it\" makes you more believable, and people trust someone who has already thought about the problems.", "do": ["Use one or two concrete examples or numbers", "Propose a small, reversible trial"], "dont": ["Exaggerate the benefits", "Hide the downsides you know about"]}, {"heading": "Draw the line between persuasion and manipulation", "body": "Ethical persuasion gives people accurate information and lets them decide freely. Manipulation tries to get a yes by going around their judgement. The difference matters at work, in friendships and especially with clients.\n\nCommon manipulative tactics to avoid: false urgency (\"Decide in the next hour or the chance is gone\" when it isn't true), fake scarcity, guilt (\"After everything I've done for you…\"), flattery to lower someone's guard, hiding important costs or risks, and wearing someone down by asking again and again until they give in.\n\nA quick test: would you be comfortable if the other person later learned exactly how and why you said what you said? If the answer is no, you have crossed the line. Persuasion that only works when people don't understand it is not persuasion.", "do": ["Give accurate information and real deadlines only", "Apply the \"would I be comfortable if they knew\" test"], "dont": ["Use guilt, false urgency or fake scarcity", "Keep asking until someone gives in from fatigue"]}, {"heading": "Listen to objections and respect the answer", "body": "Objections are useful. They tell you what is still unclear or worrying. Instead of countering immediately, ask: \"What part of this concerns you most?\" Then acknowledge it honestly — \"That's fair, the first week will be slower\" — before answering.\n\nSometimes the objection improves your idea. A colleague who says \"Our regional office can't open Google Sheets on their system\" has just saved you from a failed rollout. Adjust and thank them.\n\nFinally, accept that people may still say no. Ask if you can revisit it later with more data, and then let it go. People remember who respected their decision, and that reputation makes your next proposal far more likely to succeed.", "do": ["Ask which part concerns them most", "Adjust your idea when an objection reveals a real problem"], "dont": ["Treat every objection as an attack", "Sulk or keep pushing after a clear no"]}], "takeaways": ["Lead with what the other person cares about.", "Back your idea with specific evidence and offer a small trial.", "Be open about downsides — it makes you more believable.", "Avoid false urgency, guilt, fake scarcity and wearing people down.", "Treat objections as useful and respect a final no."], "quiz": [["Your manager cares most about accuracy. How should you open your pitch for a shared report sheet?", ["\"It will make my job easier.\"", "\"Everyone in the other departments is already using Google Sheets now.\"", "\"Trust me, it's better.\"", "\"One shared version means no more wrong numbers going out.\""], 3, "Leading with the listener's priority makes the reason relevant to them."], ["Which is the strongest piece of evidence?", ["\"Last month two reports had mismatched totals, both from people using old copies.\"", "\"Shared sheets are generally better.\"", "\"I read that most companies use them.\"", "\"I just have a feeling it will help.\""], 0, "A specific, recent example from your own team is concrete and hard to dismiss."], ["Why suggest a two-week trial instead of a permanent change?", ["It hides the downsides", "It lowers the risk of saying yes and lets results speak", "It forces people to agree", "Trials are required by policy"], 1, "Small, reversible steps are easier to accept and give real evidence."], ["Which is an example of manipulation rather than persuasion?", ["Sharing accurate costs and benefits with them", "Asking what concerns the other person", "Saying an offer ends today when it doesn't", "Proposing a trial period"], 2, "False urgency pressures people by giving them wrong information."], ["What does the \"would I be comfortable if they knew\" test check?", ["Whether your argument is long enough", "Whether you are speaking politely", "Whether you'd be honest about how you are persuading", "Whether the other side likes you and trusts your judgement"], 2, "If your approach would embarrass you once revealed, it is likely manipulative."], ["Why mention a downside of your own idea?", ["It makes you more believable and shows you've thought it through", "It guarantees a yes", "It's a useful trick to lower their guard before the real request", "It isn't needed if the idea is good"], 0, "Honesty about limits builds trust in the rest of what you say."], ["A colleague says the regional office can't open Google Sheets. What is the best response?", ["Ignore it; most people can", "Tell them they're being negative", "Push ahead anyway and fix any problems later on", "Thank them and adjust the plan to address it"], 3, "Objections can reveal real problems that would otherwise sink your idea."], ["Which of these is NOT an ethical persuasion technique?", ["Linking your idea clearly to what the other person cares about most", "Repeating the request daily until they give in from fatigue", "Offering a small trial", "Using a specific example"], 1, "Wearing someone down bypasses their judgement rather than informing it."], ["After hearing your pitch, your team lead says no. What should you do?", ["Ask whether you can revisit it later with more data, then let it go", "Raise it again tomorrow and every day after", "Complain to colleagues", "Go over her head immediately"], 0, "Respecting the decision protects your reputation for future proposals."], ["What is the best first reply to an objection?", ["\"You're wrong about this, and here's exactly why.\"", "\"That's not important.\"", "\"What part of this concerns you most?\"", "\"Let's not discuss that.\""], 2, "Understanding the concern fully comes before answering it."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "Changing the team tracker", "situation": "You want your five-person team in a Gurugram office to move from emailed Excel files to a shared tracker. Your senior colleague, Mr. Rao, says, \"The old way works fine. Why change?\"", "options": [{"text": "Say, \"Everyone is moving to shared trackers, so we should too.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Following the crowd isn't a reason that addresses his concern."}, {"text": "Ask what he likes about the current system, share last month's two mismatched reports, and suggest a two-week trial.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You understand his view, use concrete evidence and lower the risk of agreeing."}, {"text": "Send a detailed email listing ten benefits of shared trackers.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Information helps, but a long list without addressing his worry may not change his mind."}]}, {"title": "The friend who hesitates", "situation": "You want your friend Kavya to join you in a weekend course for a data skills certificate. She seems unsure and mentions her weekend family commitments. The batch fills in about two weeks.", "options": [{"text": "Tell her seats are almost gone and she must decide tonight.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Exaggerating urgency is manipulative and may damage your friendship."}, {"text": "Say, \"After all the times I've helped you, you can do this for me.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Guilt pressures her into a decision that should be hers."}, {"text": "Share what the course covers and the real deadline, ask about her weekend constraints, and say it's fine if it doesn't fit.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Accurate information and genuine respect for her choice are the heart of ethical persuasion."}, {"text": "Send her the brochure and say nothing more.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Respectful, but you miss the chance to understand and address her concern."}]}, {"title": "Budget for a new tool", "situation": "You want your manager to approve a ₹6,000-a-year subscription for a design tool. You know it will save you time, but you also know it may take a week to learn.", "options": [{"text": "Explain that it could save you about three hours a week on presentations, mention the one-week learning curve, and suggest a monthly plan first.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You link it to a business benefit, are honest about the cost and lower the risk."}, {"text": "Ask for the annual plan and don't mention the learning time.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Hiding a known downside may hurt trust when it shows up."}, {"text": "Show a quick sample slide made with the free version and ask for approval.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A sample is useful evidence, but without the business case and costs the decision is harder for your manager."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "user", "title": "Persuade, don't push", "text": "The best persuaders don't pressure anyone. They link ideas to what people care about, show evidence and respect a no. Here's how to do that — and what to avoid."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "target", "title": "Lead with their goal", "text": "Same idea, different reason — pick the one your listener cares about.", "bad": "A shared sheet will make my life easier.", "good": "One shared sheet means we stop sending wrong numbers to the regional office."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "chart", "title": "One real example beats claims", "text": "Concrete, recent evidence from your own team is hard to dismiss.", "bad": "Shared sheets are just better.", "good": "Last month two reports had mismatched totals — both from old copies."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "refresh", "title": "Make yes easy to try", "text": "Suggest a small, reversible trial and be honest about the downside. It makes you more believable.", "example": "Let's try it for two weeks. The first few days may feel slower."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "shield", "title": "Tactics that cross the line", "text": "Tap each one you'll steer clear of.", "items": ["False urgency or fake scarcity", "Guilt trips", "Hiding costs or risks", "Asking again and again until they give in"], "tip": "Test: would you be fine if they knew exactly how you persuaded them?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "search", "title": "Welcome the objection", "text": "Objections show what's still unclear — and sometimes save you from a mistake.", "bad": "You're being negative. It'll be fine.", "good": "What part of this concerns you most?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "star", "title": "Respect the final no", "text": "Ask if you can revisit it later with more data — then let it go. People remember who respected their decision."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Persuade the honest way:"}]}, {"id": "negotiating-deadlines-and-workload", "title": "Negotiating Deadlines and Workload in Your First Job", "track": "fresher", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Handle new tasks, tight deadlines and overload in your first job without saying a blunt no or quietly burning out. You will learn to clarify requests, ask your manager to prioritise and propose realistic options early.", "sections": [{"heading": "Clarify before you say yes", "body": "In a first job, it can feel risky to ask questions when your manager gives you a task. But a fast \"Yes, sir\" followed by a missed deadline is far worse than a two-minute clarifying chat.\n\nBefore you commit, check four things: what exactly is needed, by when, how good it needs to be, and how it fits with your current work. For example: \"Sure, I'll take the vendor comparison. Is it for Thursday's review? Do you need a one-page summary or the full sheet with all twelve vendors?\"\n\nThese questions often reveal that the real deadline is later, or that a short version will do. They also show your manager that you take the work seriously. Most managers prefer a fresher who asks good questions to one who guesses.", "do": ["Confirm the output, the deadline and the level of detail", "Ask how the task fits with your current work"], "dont": ["Say yes instantly and work out the details later"]}, {"heading": "When you're overloaded, ask your manager to prioritise", "body": "New joiners often take on everything because they want to impress. Then three tasks are due on the same day and something slips. The better approach is to make your workload visible and let your manager decide what comes first.\n\nKeep a simple list of your current tasks with deadlines. When a new request arrives, share it: \"I'm currently on the onboarding deck for Wednesday and the MIS report for Thursday. If I take the vendor comparison too, which of these should move?\"\n\nThis is not refusing work. It is giving your manager the information they need to make a good decision. Often they will extend one deadline, give the task to someone else or tell you a shorter version is fine — options you would never get if you stayed quiet.", "do": ["Keep an up-to-date list of tasks and deadlines", "Ask \"Which of these should move?\" instead of saying no"], "dont": ["Silently accept everything and miss deadlines", "Complain about workload without offering specifics"]}, {"heading": "Negotiate the deadline early, not at the last minute", "body": "If a deadline looks unrealistic, say so as soon as you know — ideally on the day you get the task. Asking for more time on the morning it's due damages trust; asking two days earlier with a plan usually doesn't.\n\nCome with options, not just a problem: \"I can send the analysis for the top five vendors by Thursday and the remaining seven by Monday. Would that work?\" or \"I can meet Thursday if Priya helps with the data pull.\" Options show that you are trying to solve the problem, and partial delivery often meets the real need.\n\nUse facts, not feelings. \"Collecting quotes from twelve vendors takes about three days because they reply slowly\" is more persuasive than \"It's too much.\"", "do": ["Raise deadline concerns on the day you see the risk", "Offer a partial delivery or a different scope"], "dont": ["Wait until the last day to ask for more time", "Say \"It's too much\" without explaining why"]}, {"heading": "Ask for help and keep your word", "body": "Negotiating workload also means asking for resources: access to a tool, a colleague's hour, a template from a past project. Be specific about what you need and why: \"Could I get read access to the vendor database? It would save me about a day of collecting quotes manually.\"\n\nOnce you agree on a new deadline or scope, treat it as a firm promise. Send a short confirmation message, update your task list and give a quick status if anything changes. Reliability is what earns you the trust to negotiate more freely later.\n\nFinally, protect your basic well-being. Occasional late evenings happen, but if long hours become the norm, raise it calmly with your manager using specific examples rather than letting it build into burnout.", "do": ["Ask for specific resources with a clear reason", "Confirm new deadlines in writing and meet them"], "dont": ["Agree to a new deadline and then miss it too", "Let overwork build silently until you burn out"]}], "takeaways": ["Clarify the output, deadline and detail before you say yes.", "Share your task list and ask your manager which item should move.", "Raise deadline risks early and come with options.", "Use facts, not feelings, to explain why you need more time.", "Once you agree on a new deadline, treat it as a firm promise."], "quiz": [["Your manager asks for a vendor comparison \"soon\". What should you do first?", ["Start immediately and send it whenever it's finally ready", "Ask when it's needed and how detailed it should be", "Say you're too busy", "Ask a colleague to do it"], 1, "Clarifying the deadline and level of detail avoids wasted effort and missed expectations."], ["You already have two deadlines this week and a new task arrives. Which response is best?", ["\"I can't take this.\"", "\"Sure, no problem\" — then work till midnight every night", "\"I'm on the deck for Wednesday and the MIS report for Thursday. Which should move if I take this?\"", "Accept it and quietly let one task slip"], 2, "Making your workload visible lets your manager prioritise with full information."], ["When is the best time to ask for a deadline extension?", ["As soon as you see the deadline is at risk", "On the morning it's due, once you're completely sure", "After the deadline has passed", "Never; freshers shouldn't ask"], 0, "Early warnings give your manager time to adjust and protect trust."], ["Which request for more time is most persuasive?", ["\"It's too much work.\"", "\"Everyone else gets more time.\"", "\"I'm feeling stressed.\"", "\"Vendors take about three days to reply, so I can send the top five by Thursday and the rest by Monday.\""], 3, "Facts plus a concrete option show you are solving the problem, not just reporting it."], ["Why offer a partial delivery?", ["It often meets the real need while the rest follows", "It lets you avoid the hard part of the task entirely", "It always impresses everyone", "It is required by HR"], 0, "Delivering the most important part first often satisfies the immediate requirement."], ["Which is a well-framed request for a resource?", ["\"I need more help.\"", "\"Could I get read access to the vendor database? It would save about a day.\"", "\"Why don't I have access to anything?\"", "\"Someone should help me.\""], 1, "A specific request with a clear benefit is easy to say yes to."], ["After your manager agrees to move your deadline to Monday, what should you do?", ["Assume Monday is flexible too, since your manager already moved it once", "Wait for them to remind you", "Send a short confirmation, update your task list and deliver by Monday", "Tell colleagues you got an extension"], 2, "Confirming and keeping the new promise builds the trust you need for future requests."], ["You have worked until 10 pm most nights for a month. What is the best step?", ["Keep going; it will sort itself out", "Resign without discussing it, since the culture won't change", "Complain in the team chat group", "Raise it calmly with your manager using specific examples"], 3, "A calm, specific conversation is the best way to fix a pattern before burnout."], ["Why do many managers prefer freshers who ask clarifying questions?", ["It shows they take the work seriously and reduces wasted effort", "It makes meetings longer", "It shows they don't want to work", "Managers generally enjoy answering lots of questions from new joiners"], 0, "Good questions save time and lead to better work."], ["Which statement is NOT good workload negotiation?", ["Sharing a list of current tasks and deadlines", "Offering a smaller scope to meet a tight deadline", "Asking for a specific resource with a reason", "Agreeing to every request to look committed, then missing two deadlines"], 3, "Overcommitting to impress usually leads to missed deadlines and lost trust."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "Friday afternoon request", "situation": "It's 4 pm on Friday in your first month as a management trainee in Chennai. Your manager asks you to prepare a branch performance summary \"for Monday\". You already promised the HR team a training feedback report by Monday noon.", "options": [{"text": "Say yes and plan to work all weekend without telling anyone.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "You may manage it, but hidden overwork isn't sustainable and your manager doesn't know about the clash."}, {"text": "Tell your manager about the HR report and ask, \"Should the branch summary come first, or is Monday evening okay for it?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You share the conflict and let your manager prioritise with full information."}, {"text": "Ask HR to move their deadline without checking with your manager.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "It may solve the clash, but your manager might prefer to move their own task instead."}, {"text": "Say you can't do it because you're busy.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "A flat refusal without options makes you seem unwilling rather than overloaded."}]}, {"title": "The deadline that looks impossible", "situation": "On Monday your team lead asks you to compile quotes from twelve vendors by Wednesday. From experience, you know vendors take two to three days to reply to emails.", "options": [{"text": "Wait and see; if it isn't ready Wednesday, explain then.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Raising the risk only when it's too late damages trust."}, {"text": "Today, explain the reply time and offer quotes from the five main vendors by Wednesday and the rest by Friday.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Early, fact-based and with a practical option."}, {"text": "Call all twelve vendors today to speed things up and see how far you get.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Good initiative, but you should still tell your lead about the risk in case it isn't enough."}]}, {"title": "Asking for a hand", "situation": "You are building a large Excel model for the quarterly review. Pulling raw data from the system will take you two full days, but a colleague, Priya, knows a quicker query that takes an hour. She's busy too.", "options": [{"text": "Ask Priya directly to run it for you today.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "It might work, but she is busy and may feel put on the spot; a lighter ask is better."}, {"text": "Ask Priya if she could show you the query for 15 minutes when she's free this week, so you can run it yourself.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "A small, specific ask that respects her time and builds your own skills."}, {"text": "Do it manually over two days so you don't bother anyone.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "You lose two days that a short conversation could have saved."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "briefcase", "title": "Yes, but smartly", "text": "In your first job, saying yes to everything feels safe — until three deadlines land on the same day. Learn to clarify, prioritise and ask for time without sounding unwilling."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "search", "title": "Clarify before you commit", "text": "Two minutes of questions can save two days of guessing.", "bad": "Yes sir, will do.", "good": "Sure. Is it for Thursday's review? Do you need a one-page summary or all twelve vendors?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "grid", "title": "Let your manager prioritise", "text": "Show your current work and ask which item should move. That's not refusing — it's giving them the facts.", "bad": "I can't take this, I'm too busy.", "good": "I'm on the deck for Wednesday and the MIS report for Thursday. Which should move if I take this?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "clock", "title": "Raise risks early", "text": "Ask for more time the day you spot the risk, not the morning it's due. Early warnings protect trust."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "bulb", "title": "Bring options, not just problems", "text": "Facts plus a partial delivery often meet the real need.", "bad": "It's too much work.", "good": "Vendors take about three days to reply. Top five by Thursday, the rest by Monday — would that work?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "user", "title": "Ask for specific help", "text": "Name the resource and the time it saves.", "example": "Could I get read access to the vendor database? It would save me about a day."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Once a new deadline's agreed", "text": "Tap each step as you do it.", "items": ["Send a short confirmation message", "Update your task list", "Share a quick status if anything changes", "Deliver on time"], "tip": "Long hours becoming normal? Raise it calmly, with examples."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Your workload toolkit:"}]}, {"id": "negotiating-terms-with-clients", "title": "Negotiating Fees and Terms with Clients Ethically", "track": "professional", "minutes": 8, "summary": "Discuss fees, commissions and service terms with clients openly, negotiate only what you are allowed to change, and never let a sale override suitability. You will learn phrases for fee conversations and how to record what you agree.", "sections": [{"heading": "Put every cost on the table first", "body": "Clients negotiate badly — and trust you less — when they discover costs later. Before discussing anything else, explain how you are paid and what the client will pay. If you are a distributor earning commission from the product provider, say so plainly. If you charge an advisory fee, explain the amount, how it is calculated and when it is billed.\n\nAlso cover the product's own costs: the expense ratio of a fund, any exit load and its time period, transaction charges or account fees. A simple line helps: \"Here are all the costs you'll bear, in one place. Please ask me about anything that isn't clear.\"\n\nPut this in a short written note the client can keep. Transparency doesn't weaken your position; it removes suspicion so the real conversation can happen.", "do": ["Explain how you are paid before recommending anything", "Give the client all costs in writing, in one place"], "dont": ["Leave commissions or charges for the client to discover later", "Use jargon to make costs sound smaller"]}, {"heading": "Know what is and isn't negotiable", "body": "Some things are yours to negotiate; others are not. Product costs set by a fund house, statutory charges and regulatory requirements such as KYC and risk profiling are not open for bargaining, and pretending otherwise misleads the client.\n\nWhat you can usually shape is your own service: how often you review the portfolio, whether meetings are in person or on video, how quickly you respond, what reports you send and — where your business model and rules allow — your own advisory fee. Offering service tiers is often fairer than cutting your fee: \"For ₹X a year, I review quarterly and meet twice. If you prefer, a half-yearly review costs less.\"\n\nIf a client asks for a discount you can't give, say so clearly and explain why, rather than making vague promises or quietly cutting corners on service later.", "do": ["Offer service tiers that match different needs", "Say clearly when something is not negotiable and why"], "dont": ["Offer to \"adjust\" regulated charges or skip required steps", "Agree to a fee you can't sustain and then cut service quietly"]}, {"heading": "Never trade suitability for a sale", "body": "The hardest pressure often comes from clients themselves: \"Skip the risk questionnaire, I know what I want\", \"Just put everything in the fund that gave 40% last year\" or \"I'll invest only if you promise 15%.\" Agreeing might close the deal today, but it is wrong and can hurt the client.\n\nHold the line politely and explain why it protects them: \"I need to understand your goals and comfort with risk before I can recommend anything — it's there to protect you.\" On returns, be direct: \"No one can promise returns on market-linked investments. I can show you how this fund has behaved in good and bad years.\"\n\nIf a client insists on something unsuitable, document your advice and their decision, or decline the business. Losing one sale is better than a product that doesn't fit and a complaint later.", "do": ["Complete risk profiling before any recommendation", "Say plainly that returns can't be promised"], "dont": ["Promise or hint at guaranteed returns", "Recommend a product because it closes the sale faster or pays more commission"]}, {"heading": "Agree terms in writing and review them", "body": "Once you and the client agree, summarise it in writing: the services you will provide, the fee or commission arrangement, review frequency, how to reach you and how either side can end the arrangement. Ask the client to confirm.\n\nRevisit the terms at least once a year or when the client's situation changes — a new job, marriage, a child, retirement. A client who first wanted monthly calls may now prefer a half-yearly review, or may need more support than before.\n\nWhen terms change, write that down too. A clear record protects the client, protects you, and makes it easy for anyone in your firm to see what was promised. Long-term client relationships rest less on any single negotiation than on a track record of doing exactly what you said.", "do": ["Send a written summary of services, fees and review frequency", "Review terms when the client's life changes"], "dont": ["Change terms verbally without a record"]}], "takeaways": ["Explain how you are paid and every cost before recommending anything.", "Negotiate your own service, not regulated charges or required steps.", "Offer service tiers rather than quiet cuts in quality.", "Never skip suitability or promise returns to close a sale.", "Put agreed terms in writing and review them as life changes."], "quiz": [["When should you explain how you are paid?", ["Only if the client asks you directly about it", "Before recommending any product", "After the client invests", "Never; it confuses clients"], 1, "Disclosing your payment upfront lets the client judge your advice with full information."], ["Which of these is usually open for you to negotiate?", ["A fund's expense ratio", "KYC and documentation requirements for new clients", "How often you review the portfolio", "Statutory charges"], 2, "Your own service level is yours to shape; product costs and rules are not."], ["A client asks for a lower advisory fee you can't sustain. What's the most ethical response?", ["Explain your fee clearly and offer a lighter service tier at a lower price", "Agree, then reduce your service quietly", "Promise extra returns to justify the fee", "Refuse to discuss fees at all"], 0, "Service tiers keep the arrangement honest and sustainable for both sides."], ["A client says, \"Skip the risk questionnaire, I know what I want.\" What should you do?", ["Skip it to keep the client happy", "Fill it in yourself using your best guesses about their goals", "Ask a colleague to sign it", "Explain it protects them, then complete it before advising"], 3, "Suitability comes first, regardless of pressure to close quickly."], ["A prospect will invest only if you promise 15% a year. What is the right answer?", ["\"I can guarantee 15% if you stay invested.\"", "\"No one can promise returns on market-linked products; let me show how this fund has behaved in good and bad years.\"", "\"Most of my clients get more than that.\"", "\"Let's just say 15% is likely.\""], 1, "Returns can't be promised; showing past behaviour honestly is the responsible approach."], ["Which is the best way to share all costs with a client?", ["Mention them quickly at the end of the meeting", "Wait for the client to find them in the documents", "Give a short written note listing every cost in one place", "Describe them as \"very small\" without giving any actual figures"], 2, "A clear, written list removes suspicion and helps the client decide."], ["A client insists on a high-risk product that doesn't fit their profile. What is appropriate?", ["Document your advice and their decision, or decline the business", "Process it and say nothing", "Change their risk profile to match the product", "Tell them it's actually low risk"], 0, "Recording advice — or stepping back — protects the client and keeps you honest."], ["Which item does NOT belong in a written summary of agreed terms?", ["Services you will provide", "Fee or commission arrangement", "Review frequency", "An expected return figure"], 3, "Agreed terms describe your service and costs, not promised returns."], ["A long-time client has just retired. What should you do about your terms?", ["Nothing; terms are permanent", "Review the services and frequency to match their new needs", "How often you will review the portfolio and meet the client", "End the relationship"], 1, "Life changes are a natural trigger to revisit what the client needs."], ["Why can full fee transparency strengthen your negotiating position?", ["It lets you charge whatever you want without any questions", "Clients stop asking questions", "It removes suspicion so you can discuss value openly", "It makes regulated charges negotiable"], 2, "Trust lets the conversation focus on the service and its value."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The discount request", "situation": "Mrs. Iyer, a retired teacher in Coimbatore, likes your advisory service but says your annual fee is higher than her nephew's adviser. She asks you to \"match it, and you can just meet me less often\".", "options": [{"text": "Agree to match the fee and quietly reduce attention to her portfolio.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Cutting service without saying so is misleading and may harm her."}, {"text": "Explain what your fee covers, and offer a clearly defined lighter tier — for example, half-yearly reviews at a lower fee — in writing.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "It is transparent, sustainable and lets her choose the service she wants."}, {"text": "Politely say your fee is fixed and she's welcome to compare.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Honest, but you miss a chance to offer a fair option that might suit her."}]}, {"title": "\"Just put it in last year's winner\"", "situation": "A young IT professional, Rahul, wants to invest ₹5 lakh, his entire emergency savings, in a sectoral fund that rose sharply last year. He says, \"Don't waste time on forms, I'll sign whatever.\" The commission on this fund is higher than on others.", "options": [{"text": "Process it; he's an adult and asked for it.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Ignoring suitability — and the commission conflict — puts the sale above his interests."}, {"text": "Complete his risk profile, explain why emergency money needs to stay accessible, disclose your commission, and discuss more suitable options.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Suitability, disclosure and honest advice come before closing the sale."}, {"text": "Suggest he invest half in the sectoral fund and half elsewhere.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "It reduces risk, but skips the risk profile and still uses emergency money for a volatile product."}, {"text": "Refuse to help him at all.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Walking away without advising misses the chance to guide him to something suitable."}]}, {"title": "The surprise exit load", "situation": "A client calls, upset that he was charged an exit load when he redeemed after eight months. He says no one told him. Your records show it was mentioned in the scheme documents but not in your meeting notes.", "options": [{"text": "Tell him it was in the documents and he should have read them.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Technically true, but it ignores the gap in your own explanation and damages trust."}, {"text": "Apologise that it wasn't made clear, explain how it was calculated, and start giving every client a one-page written cost summary.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "You own the gap, explain clearly and fix the process for the future."}, {"text": "Apologise and explain the exit load calculation.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A fair response, but it doesn't fix the process that caused the surprise."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "shield", "title": "Fair fees build lasting clients", "text": "Clients forgive a fee they understood. They don't forgive one they discovered. Learn to talk about costs openly, negotiate the right things and never trade suitability for a sale."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "info", "title": "Disclose how you're paid, first", "text": "Say it before you recommend anything — commission or fee.", "bad": "Don't worry about charges, they're very small.", "good": "Here's how I'm paid and every cost you'll bear, in one place. Ask me about anything unclear."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Your one-page cost note", "text": "Tap each item once it's covered.", "items": ["How you are paid", "Fund expense ratio", "Exit load and its period", "Transaction or account charges"], "tip": "Give it in writing so the client can keep it."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "grid", "title": "Negotiate service, not rules", "text": "Product costs, KYC and risk profiling aren't yours to bargain. Review frequency, meeting format and your own fee — where allowed — can be shaped."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "star", "title": "Offer tiers, not quiet cuts", "text": "If you can't lower the fee, offer a clearly defined lighter service.", "bad": "Okay, I'll match it. (Then meets her less without saying so.)", "good": "For this fee I review quarterly. A half-yearly review costs less — shall I write both down?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "flag", "title": "Never promise returns", "text": "Not even to close a big sale. Show past behaviour honestly instead.", "bad": "Stay invested and 15% is practically guaranteed.", "good": "No one can promise market returns. Here's how this fund behaved in good and bad years."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "book", "title": "Write it down, review it", "text": "Summarise services, fees, review frequency and exit terms in writing. Revisit when life changes — new job, marriage, retirement."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Your ethical fee checklist:"}]}];
